WEBVTT

1
. --> .
Before we touch the worked example, notice how one extra word can change a quantity completely.

2
. --> .
If I say twelve metres, I have told you a size.

3
. --> .
If I say twelve metres east, I have told you a size and a direction.

4
. --> .
That extra direction is not a side note.

5
. --> .
It changes the kind of quantity we are talking about.

6
. --> .
This is why physics separates scalars from vectors.

7
. --> .
Some quantities answer how much only.

8
. --> .
Others answer how much and which way.

9
. --> .
Once that distinction is clear, distance and displacement stop colliding, and speed and velocity stop sounding interchangeable.

10
. --> .
Here is the cleanest test for this lesson.

11
. --> .
When a quantity appears, ask the first question: how much?

12
. --> .
If that is enough, the quantity is scalar.

13
. --> .
Then ask the second question: which way?

14
. --> .
If you need direction as well, the quantity is vector.

15
. --> .
Mass does not need a direction.

16
. --> .
Time does not need a direction.

17
. --> .
Temperature does not need a direction.

18
. --> .
But displacement, velocity, force, and acceleration all need direction before the description is complete.

19
. --> .
That simple two-question check is the lens you want to keep in your head while the worked example unfolds.

20
. --> .
One of the easiest ways to feel a vector is to picture an arrow on a map.

21
. --> .
The arrow has a length, and that length represents magnitude.

22
. --> .
But it also points in a particular direction, and that direction is part of the information.

23
. --> .
If you rotate the arrow, you have changed the vector even when the length stays the same.

24
. --> .
That is why vectors cannot be described by magnitude alone.

25
. --> .
Displacement and force behave like this.

26
. --> .
They are not finished until the direction is attached.

27
. --> .
Now compare that with a scalar.

28
. --> .
A thermometer reading tells how hot or cold something is, but it does not point north, south, east, or west.

29
. --> .
A balance reading tells how much mass something has, but again there is no direction attached.

30
. --> .
Time behaves the same way.

31
. --> .
Twenty seconds is complete without any arrow.

32
. --> .
So scalars are size-only quantities.

33
. --> .
They can be large or small, but they do not carry a built-in direction.

34
. --> .
This is where many learners get tripped up.

35
. --> .
A vector of six metres east and a vector of six metres north have the same magnitude, but they are not the same vector.

36
. --> .
The size matches, yet the direction changes the meaning.

37
. --> .
That same idea explains the difference between speed and velocity.

38
. --> .
An object can keep the same speed while turning around, and when that happens the velocity changes because direction changed.

39
. --> .
So whenever direction matters, you must read it as part of the quantity, not as optional decoration.

40
. --> .
Now apply the idea to motion language.

41
. --> .
Distance is scalar because it asks how much route was covered.

42
. --> .
It follows the full path.

43
. --> .
Displacement is vector because it asks for the start-to-finish change in position, and that answer needs direction.

44
. --> .
So if a learner walks out and then partly back, distance keeps counting the whole route, while displacement compares where the learner ended up with where the learner began.

45
. --> .
The two quantities can describe the same journey, but they answer different questions.

46
. --> .
Take an out-and-back route.

47
. --> .
Suppose a learner walks ten metres east and then four metres west.

48
. --> .
The total route travelled is fourteen metres, so the distance is fourteen metres.

49
. --> .
But the finishing point is only six metres east of the start, so the displacement is six metres east.

50
. --> .
That single journey already shows why distance and displacement are different ideas.

51
. --> .
One keeps the whole route.

52
. --> .
The other keeps the net change with direction.

53
. --> .
Pause and check your own thinking.

54
. --> .
If a quantity needs only size, what is it?

55
. --> .
It is scalar.

56
. --> .
If a quantity needs size and direction, what is it?

57
. --> .
It is vector.

58
. --> .
Which description must be vector: six metres or six metres north?

59
. --> .
Six metres north, because direction is attached.

60
. --> .
And if a journey goes out and then partly back, which quantity follows the full route?

61
. --> .
Distance does.

62
. --> .
Which quantity follows the start-to-finish arrow?

63
. --> .
Displacement does.

64
. --> .
You are now ready for the worked example in this lesson.

65
. --> .
The guided example starts with a delivery robot moving twelve metres east.

66
. --> .
Read it in two passes.

67
. --> .
First, notice the magnitude: twelve metres.

68
. --> .
Second, notice the direction: east.

69
. --> .
Once both pieces are visible, the classification is straightforward.

70
. --> .
A description with magnitude and direction is vector.

71
. --> .
If that logic already feels natural, the worked example will feel like confirmation rather than a guess.
